---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Learning Rust while the agents write it — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="I picked Rust when I knew only the basics, and I still write very little of it by hand. Here is the process that I run around the agents, and the four ways that it goes wrong."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-07-02">July 2, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Learning Rust while the agents write it</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          I picked Rust when I knew only the fundamentals.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 6 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          My real job is the process around the agents. I write the plan, and
          an agent improves it. A second agent builds it, one task at a time. A
          third agent reviews the code, runs it, and reads the logs. Then I run
          the app and see it work.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <figure class="lp-figure lp-try" id="batch-demo">
        <div class="lp-try-controls">
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-mode="one" aria-pressed="true">One task at a time</button>
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-mode="all" aria-pressed="false">All five at once</button>
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-run>Run</button>
        </div>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 128" role="img" aria-label="Five tasks land one at a time. The app runs after task 1 and task 2. It breaks after task 3, so task 3 is the only suspect. Tasks 4 and 5 wait.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">FIVE TASKS. ONE OF THEM HAS A BUG.</text>
          <g data-task>
            <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="1" y="28" width="110" height="56" rx="6"></rect>
            <text class="lp-fig-t" x="56" y="51" text-anchor="middle">Task 1</text>
            <text class="lp-fig-s" x="56" y="71" text-anchor="middle">built</text>
            <text class="lp-fig-s lp-fig-good" x="56" y="106" text-anchor="middle">app runs</text>
          </g>
          <g data-task>
            <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="133" y="28" width="110" height="56" rx="6"></rect>
            <text class="lp-fig-t" x="188" y="51" text-anchor="middle">Task 2</text>
            <text class="lp-fig-s" x="188" y="71" text-anchor="middle">built</text>
            <text class="lp-fig-s lp-fig-good" x="188" y="106" text-anchor="middle">app runs</text>
          </g>
          <g data-task>
            <rect class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" x="265" y="28" width="110" height="56" rx="6"></rect>
            <text class="lp-fig-t" x="320" y="51" text-anchor="middle">Task 3</text>
            <text class="lp-fig-s" x="320" y="71" text-anchor="middle">suspect</text>
            <text class="lp-fig-s lp-fig-bad" x="320" y="106" text-anchor="middle">app breaks</text>
          </g>
          <g data-task>
            <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="397" y="28" width="110" height="56" rx="6"></rect>
            <text class="lp-fig-t" x="452" y="51" text-anchor="middle">Task 4</text>
            <text class="lp-fig-s" x="452" y="71" text-anchor="middle">waiting</text>
            <text class="lp-fig-s" x="452" y="106" text-anchor="middle"></text>
          </g>
          <g data-task>
            <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="529" y="28" width="110" height="56" rx="6"></rect>
            <text class="lp-fig-t" x="584" y="51" text-anchor="middle">Task 5</text>
            <text class="lp-fig-s" x="584" y="71" text-anchor="middle">waiting</text>
            <text class="lp-fig-s" x="584" y="106" text-anchor="middle"></text>
          </g>
          <g data-batch opacity="0">
            <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M1 92 V98 H639 V92"></path>
            <text class="lp-fig-s lp-fig-bad" x="320" y="118" text-anchor="middle" data-batch-text>one build, one run: app breaks</text>
          </g>
        </svg>
        <p class="lp-try-readout" aria-live="polite">Task 3 broke the app. App runs: 3. Suspects: 1.</p>
        <figcaption>
          Pick how the five tasks land, then press Run. One task at a time
          costs more runs, but it tells me which task broke the app.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>I took on two problems at once</h2>
      <p>
        When I <a href="/blogs/why-rust.html">chose Rust for the whole app</a>,
        I knew the basics. That is very far from image code that keeps up when
        you drag a slider. The second problem is the goal: the image must
        change as soon as you move a slider, also in a folder with thousands
        of RAW files. It is very hard to add speed at the end. Many side
        projects stall with both problems at once. I think that mine did not
        stall because I do not type the code.
      </p>

      <h2>Where my time goes</h2>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 230" role="img" aria-label="Stacked bars for five stages of the process. I do almost all of the planning work myself. The agents do almost all of the build and release work. Testing and review are in the middle: the agents do much of the work, and I check it.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">WHO DOES THE WORK, BY STAGE</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="44">Plan</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="96" y="30" width="470" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="566" y="30" width="42" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-on-bar" x="104" y="44">me, and I do not hand this one over</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="80">Build</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="96" y="66" width="40" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="136" y="66" width="472" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="144" y="80">the agents write almost all of it</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="116">Test</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="96" y="102" width="240" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="336" y="102" width="272" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-on-bar" x="104" y="116">me, in the real app</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="152">Review</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="96" y="138" width="180" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="276" y="138" width="332" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-on-bar" x="104" y="152">me, reading the diff</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="188">Release</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="96" y="174" width="24" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="120" y="174" width="488" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="128" y="188">scripted, and safe to run twice</text>

          <line class="lp-fig-line-soft" x1="96" y1="204" x2="608" y2="204" stroke-dasharray="3 4"></line>
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="96" y="222">BLUE IS THE WORK THAT I DO WITH MY OWN HANDS</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          The agents do nearly all of the typing. I keep the planning for
          myself, because everything after it depends on a correct plan.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Planning is the part that I do not hand over</h2>
      <p>
        Every change starts as a paragraph and a list that I write myself. It
        says what the change is, which parts of the app it touches, what I
        leave out on purpose, and how I will know that it worked.
      </p>
      <p>
        That last item does most of the work. Without it, the agent picks its
        own idea of "done", and that idea is generous to itself. "Make
        thumbnails faster" is too vague to check. This target is not: "A
        folder of 3,000 photos fills the first two screens, the scroll does
        not stutter, and memory does not climb while I scroll."
      </p>
      <p>
        A plan is also the cheapest place to be wrong. A wrong idea in a
        paragraph costs me a minute. In finished code, it costs an afternoon,
        and by then it compiles and I am attached to it. This is the habit of
        the seven-item list in
        <a href="/blogs/why-i-started-lightphotos.html">the first post</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Building one thing at a time</h2>
      <p>
        Every change must leave the app in a working state. I build and run
        the app after each task. When two changes land together and the app
        misbehaves, I cannot tell which one caused it. Agents write a lot of
        code very quickly, and they bundle changes if I let them.
      </p>

      <h2>I do not take the agent's word for it</h2>
      <p>
        Agents are quick to announce success: "The thumbnail loading should
        now be much faster." That word "should" cost me more time than any bug
        in this codebase. I do not count a number until I see it myself.
      </p>
      <p>
        The pixel maths has real tests. Exposure, the tone curve, white
        balance, and the other adjustments are pure functions: the same input
        always gives the same output. So I check them against fixed numbers.
      </p>
      <p>
        I check scrolling, decoding, panning, and memory when I run the app.
        This is my weakest area. I can see if something is broken, but not if
        a change made the app faster or slower, because I never click the
        same way twice. I want to fix that next.
      </p>

      <h2>Getting a second agent to review</h2>
      <p>
        When a change is done, a fresh agent with no memory of the work
        reviews the diff. This helped more than I expected. The agent that
        just wrote four hundred lines is a poor judge of them, and it agrees
        with whatever I suggest.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 200" role="img" aria-label="Two reviewers compared. The agent that wrote the code agrees with you warmly, and it cannot judge its own four hundred lines. A fresh agent with no memory finds the same five things again and again: a clone to avoid the borrow checker, an allocation in a per-pixel loop, an unwrap on user input, a lock held during slow work, and a refactor that nobody asked for.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">WHO SHOULD REVIEW THE DIFF</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="24" width="300" height="168" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="46">The agent that wrote it</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" x="16" y="58" width="268" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="150" y="78" text-anchor="middle">Agrees with you, warmly</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="112">It just wrote four hundred lines.</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="132">It is the worst possible judge</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="152">of whether they are any good.</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="320" y="24" width="320" height="168" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="336" y="46">A fresh agent, no memory</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="336" y="62" width="7" height="7" rx="1"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="352" y="70">A clone to avoid the borrow checker</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="336" y="84" width="7" height="7" rx="1"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="352" y="92">An allocation in a per-pixel loop</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="336" y="106" width="7" height="7" rx="1"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="352" y="114">An unwrap on user input</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="336" y="128" width="7" height="7" rx="1"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="352" y="136">A lock held during slow work</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="336" y="150" width="7" height="7" rx="1"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="352" y="158">A refactor that nobody asked for</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          The fresh reviewer finds the same kinds of problems again and again.
          Most of them are about wasted work. Few of them are real bugs.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Rust compiles all five without a warning. A clone (a copy) is the
        easiest way to satisfy the rules about who owns each piece of memory.
        On a 60 MB image in a function that runs every frame, that copy is a
        real problem. A new allocation in a per-pixel loop runs twenty million
        times. A lock held during slow work can freeze the interface.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then I read the diff myself. I rarely catch what the reviewer missed,
        but reading diffs is how I learned Rust.
      </p>

      <h2>Keeping releases dull on purpose</h2>
      <p>
        A script bumps the version, builds each platform, and updates the
        site. It is safe to run twice by accident. If a release takes
        concentration, I put it off, and then nobody can use a month of work.
      </p>

      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Stage</th>
              <th scope="col">What I hand over</th>
              <th scope="col">Done when</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Plan</th>
              <td>A paragraph and a list</td>
              <td>I can state what would prove it</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Build</th>
              <td>One task, with its scope</td>
              <td>It compiles and the app still runs</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Test</th>
              <td>The proof from the plan</td>
              <td>I saw it, on a real folder</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Review</th>
              <td>The diff, to an agent that did not write it</td>
              <td>Findings are fixed or consciously declined</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Release</th>
              <td>A version number</td>
              <td>The build on the site is the build I tested</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Four ways this goes wrong</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Fixing symptoms.</strong> The agent patches one symptom, then
        the next one. After four rounds, the code is full of special-case
        checks, and nobody has said what the real problem is. Now I stop and
        reproduce the problem, and the agent names the cause before it makes
        one fix.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Confident numbers that nobody measured.</strong> An agent once
        told me that a change made something "roughly three times faster". It
        did not run anything. A number that nobody measured is a guess.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Code that is correct but wasteful.</strong> Rust's compiler
        checks that your code does nothing unsafe with memory. It does not
        care if you copied fifty megabytes for no reason. Speed is the point
        of this app, so a change must pass a speed check before I accept it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The change keeps growing.</strong> A change picks up work that
        nobody asked for. The out-of-scope list in the plan stops most of it,
        and I catch the rest in the diff.
      </p>

      <h2>How I learned Rust</h2>
      <p>
        Four things taught me, in approximate order of value.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Reading diffs and asking why.</strong> I asked "Why is this
          line here?" a few hundred times, about code that I care about.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Asking the agent to explain its choices.</strong> Why a
          clone here? Why a reference there? Why <code>Arc</code> (a way to
          share one value between threads) and not a plain value? The answers
          made the ownership rules predictable.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Compiler errors.</strong> The borrow checker enforces the
          ownership rules, and it tells you exactly where you broke them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Writing some of it by hand anyway.</strong> I write one
          function per session, with no agent. It is slow, but it shows what I
          understand and what I only recognise.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A few weeks in, I can read all of the Rust in LightPhotos and write
        most of it. Reading matters most. An agent can always write more code
        than I can, but it cannot tell me if that code is good. If I cannot
        tell good code from code that only looks right, I will get an app that
        only works, not the app that I want.
      </p>


      <nav class="lp-article-nav" aria-label="More posts">
        <a class="lp-article-nav-prev" href="/blogs/why-rust.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Back</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">Why Rust</span>
        </a>
        <a class="lp-article-nav-next" href="/blogs/how-a-photo-gets-on-screen.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Next</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">How a photo gets on screen</span>
        </a>
      </nav>
    </article>
  </main>
  <script>
    const fig = document.getElementById("batch-demo");
    if (fig) {
      const tasks = [...fig.querySelectorAll("[data-task]")];
      const modeBtns = [...fig.querySelectorAll("[data-mode]")];
      const runBtn = fig.querySelector("[data-run]");
      const batch = fig.querySelector("[data-batch]");
      const svg = fig.querySelector("svg");
      const readout = fig.querySelector(".lp-try-readout");
      const reduce = window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)");
      const LOOK = {
        wait: ["lp-fig-box", "waiting", "", ""],
        built: ["lp-fig-box-accent", "built", "", ""],
        ok: ["lp-fig-box-accent", "built", "app runs", "lp-fig-good"],
        bad: ["lp-fig-bad-stroke", "suspect", "app breaks", "lp-fig-bad"],
        suspect: ["lp-fig-bad-stroke", "suspect", "", ""],
      };
      let mode = "one";
      let timer = 0;

      const setTask = (i, state) => {
        const [box, status, check, tone] = LOOK[state];
        const [rect, , statusEl, checkEl] = tasks[i].children;
        rect.setAttribute("class", box);
        statusEl.textContent = status;
        checkEl.textContent = check;
        checkEl.setAttribute("class", ("lp-fig-s " + tone).trim());
      };

      // Each frame is a list of task states, a batch line flag and a readout.
      const frames = (bug) => {
        const out = [];
        if (mode === "one") {
          for (let i = 0; i <= bug; i++) {
            const states = tasks.map((_, j) => (j < i ? "ok" : j === i ? (i === bug ? "bad" : "ok") : "wait"));
            const text = i === bug
              ? `Task ${bug + 1} broke the app. App runs: ${bug + 1}. Suspects: 1.`
              : `Task ${i + 1} built. The app runs.`;
            out.push([states, false, text]);
          }
        } else {
          for (let i = 0; i < tasks.length; i++) {
            out.push([tasks.map((_, j) => (j <= i ? "built" : "wait")), false, `Task ${i + 1} built. No run yet.`]);
          }
          out.push([tasks.map(() => "suspect"), true, "The app broke. App runs: 1. Suspects: 5, tasks 1 to 5."]);
        }
        return out;
      };

      const show = ([states, batchOn, text]) => {
        states.forEach((s, i) => setTask(i, s));
        batch.setAttribute("opacity", batchOn ? "1" : "0");
        readout.textContent = text;
        svg.setAttribute("aria-label", text);
      };

      const stop = () => clearTimeout(timer);

      const run = () => {
        stop();
        const list = frames(Math.floor(Math.random() * tasks.length));
        if (reduce.matches) return show(list[list.length - 1]);
        let k = 0;
        const step = () => {
          show(list[k++]);
          if (k < list.length) timer = setTimeout(step, 450);
        };
        step();
      };

      modeBtns.forEach((btn) => {
        btn.addEventListener("click", () => {
          stop();
          mode = btn.dataset.mode;
          modeBtns.forEach((b) => b.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(b === btn)));
          show([tasks.map(() => "wait"), false,
            mode === "one" ? "Press Run to land the tasks one at a time." : "Press Run to land all five tasks, then run the app once."]);
        });
      });
      runBtn.addEventListener("click", run);
    }
  </script>
</Base>
